 If `harvey.local.yaml` exists it wins. It's gitignored, so a fork can carry real product configuration while the tracked `harvey.yaml` stays a template — nobody publishes their positioning and pricing by accident.
 
+### Bounded local-outreach pilot (opt-in)
+
+Existing installations keep their discovery and verification choices unless
+`prospecting.enabled` is true. Prepare this in the private `harvey.local.yaml`,
+with sending paused and `require_approval: true`; configuration alone is not
+permission to activate autopilot or evidence that a mailbox is deliverable.
+
+```yaml
+prospecting:
+  enabled: false
+  provider: tinyfish                 # tinyfish or explicitly selected osm only
+  max_daily_queries: 10
+  results_per_query: 10
+  refill_below: 10
+  verification_provider: zerobounce
+  max_monthly_verifications: 100
+  published_email_only: true
+  postcode_prefixes: ["65"]
+channels:
+  email:
+    provider: gmail
+    require_approval: true
+    max_daily_sends: 20
+    max_daily_new_contacts: 5
+    send_to_risky: false
+    send_window:
+      start: "09:00"
+      end: "17:00"
+      weekdays_only: true
+      recipient_timezone: false
+usage:
+  heartbeat_interval_minutes: 15
+  quiet_hours: {start: "17:00", end: "09:00", timezone: Europe/Paris}
+```
+
+Use a real dedicated secondary-domain mailbox, its actual ramp start date
+(`min_days: 14`, `start_sends: 5`, `daily_increase: 1`), confirmed signals and a
+reviewed do-not-contact list. A ramp is a sending restriction, not proof of
+reputation; no paid warmup service is required by this pilot. Keep the chosen
+Claude subscription provider; there is no silent model or paid API fallback.
+
+Discovery rotates one industry/place pair per batch. Every provider request,
+including retries and OSM geocoding, reserves the durable daily budget first.
+ZeroBounce reserves a UTC-month budget before verifying any MX type; cached
+conclusive results are reused. A failed or ambiguous request is not refunded.
+Missing keys, exhausted budgets and unavailable providers remain visible waits,
+not “no prospects” or permission to use a paid fallback.
+
+An address being deliverable does not establish permission to contact it.
+`outreach_eligibility` records exact website quotations for the published address,
+local postcode and configured commune, established small-business size and relevant
+activity, with source URLs and collection/check dates. A postal-looking amount
+or service area does not prove an establishment. Missing or ambiguous evidence
+goes to human review. Imports and discovered contacts use this same admission
+rule; the first send checks it again. Contacts displays the reasons and evidence.
+
+The enabled pilot accepts exactly three complete sequence steps, with relative
+delays `[0,4,6]`. Followups use the real first send at J+4/J+10, move into the next
+business window and wait at least a day after the previous actual send. First
+contacts and all-email limits use the operator's local day; the first-contact cap
+does not block followups or replies. The final post-claim window check also
+applies to replies. Ambiguous sends are retained for human investigation, never
+automatically retried.
+
+`compliance.information_line` adds a deterministic information notice before the
+opt-out line. It appears in draft previews and counts towards the global message
+limit. Only assert a published-address origin after qualification establishes it.
+French refusals, including a standalone STOP with a signature, suppress future
+outreach without acknowledgement. Deletion/opposition requests also reach human
+review; other legal/data-rights and commercial exceptions remain held. Native
+reply conversation/outbox/action/processed writes commit together, so a database
+failure is retryable without advancing the Gmail cursor or duplicating an answer.
+The bounce pause uses a historical ratio after ten sends, not a sliding window;
+`sending resume` resets its counter and is not a substitute for reviewing bounces.
+
+Real appointments are read from Calendly's free v2 API, not inferred from a
+positive reply or conversation stage. Put `CALENDLY_API_TOKEN` privately in `.env`
+with `users:read`, `event_types:read` and `scheduled_events:read`, and configure the
+exact `product.offer.booking_url`. Only the matching event type and exact email
+of an actually contacted prospect qualify. Complete pagination is required;
+missing/invalid access or an incomplete snapshot blocks cold outreach via
+`calendar_sync_status`, shown on Today. The client uses only GET requests and
+never creates, moves or cancels events. No webhook or public callback is needed.
+
+`calendar_bookings` supplies appointment dates and API provenance to Contacts and
+Conversations. `meeting` / `meeting_booked` / `closed_won` mean an appointment here,
+not a completed sale. Booking cancels pending commercial mail; cancellation with
+no other active appointment escalates without restarting outreach. Operators
+handle rights requests and retention/purges; the pilot creates no automatic
+irreversible deletion engine. Validate real provider access, controlled mailbox
+delivery and a real test reservation before enabling autonomy.
+
+### Dashboard restart intention and recovery
+
+A fresh dashboard never starts the heartbeat by itself. Start persists
+`heartbeat_enabled=true` only after the owned child has a live PID and the native
+loop lock. The dashboard lifespan resumes that explicit intention after restart,
+using the same setup guard; incomplete setup stays visibly blocked without
+discarding the intention. Stop persists `false` before signalling, so a restart
+does not undo a voluntary stop. A CLI-owned loop is observed, not duplicated or
+terminated by the dashboard.
+
+Stop returns immediately with `phase: stopping`; Start stays disabled while the
+single shared drain finishes. The owned child receives one SIGTERM and gets up
+to 30 minutes to finish current work before an explicitly logged forced stop.
+Application shutdown waits for that drain without disabling restart intention.
+An unexpected owned-child exit terminates the dashboard nonzero for the container
+supervisor; there is no internal respawn loop. The opt-in pilot drains long agent
+work rather than applying the legacy 600-second cycle watchdog.
+
+Controls and Today separate process phase, restart intention,
+`heartbeat_last_cycle_at` (UTC completed cycle), and `heartbeat_phase` /
+`heartbeat_blocked_reason`. A reachable dashboard is not proof of a healthy
+heartbeat; a long in-progress operation is not a completed cycle.
+
+Before migration, pause sending, stop the heartbeat gracefully, then stop the app.
+Back up the entire private workspace, including hidden provider homes, configuration
+and SQLite/WAL, not just a live database file. On an isolated restore, disable
+`heartbeat_enabled` and set `sending_paused` through `StateManager`, and require
+approval in configuration **before the first app startup**. Verify SQLite integrity,
+private login-directory permissions, exclusions, quotas and proof records without
+printing credentials. An offline restore does not prove a native encrypted-backup
+restore, real container/host reboot, mailbox delivery or provider authentication.
+
